  values ('ME', 'long_answer', 'As a democratic nation, there is a chance that we stand divided in thoughts and actions, as we have the right to exercise our votes. In the last three elections we have seen fragmentation and party cleavages. Such divisions, if allowed to proliferate, might breach the sacred bond between the three important entities (Tsawa Sum) - the King, Country and People - in the longer run.
How can you keep the sacred bond between the King, Country and People ever strong, vibrant and uncorrupted as we advance into a modern economy and democracy? Discuss. (20 marks)', 'Tsawa Sum - the King, the Country and the People - is the sacred bond at the heart of Bhutan''s identity, sovereignty and security. Democracy gives citizens the right to choose, but elections have sometimes divided families, villages and communities along party lines, and social media has amplified quarrels. If these divisions grow, they could weaken unity and trust.
Threats: party politics extending into personal and community relationships; regionalism and narrow interests; misinformation and hateful content online; corruption and inequality that erode trust; and materialism and individualism that weaken shared values.
How to keep the bond strong:
1. Keep the Monarchy and key institutions above politics: the King as a unifying symbol, an apolitical National Council and impartial civil service, judiciary and armed forces.
2. Strengthen election rules and their enforcement so campaigns stay issue-based and respectful, and parties do not divide communities.
3. Civic and values education in schools, colleges, Gyalsung and the media on Tsawa Sum, the Constitution, citizens'' duties and the meaning of democracy - that differences end on election day.
4. Fight corruption and ensure fair, inclusive development so that all citizens and regions feel valued.
5. National service and volunteerism (De-suung, Gyalsung) that bring people from all backgrounds together in service of the nation.
6. Responsible media and social media use, countering misinformation and hate.
Personally, I would vote based on issues, respect people with different views, avoid spreading divisive content, remain united with my community after elections, serve the country with integrity, and remember that loyalty to Tsawa Sum is above loyalty to any party.', null, '[{"point": "Explains Tsawa Sum and its importance (unity, sovereignty, loyalty, foundation of Bhutanese identity)", "marks": 3}, {"point": "Threats: party divisions in families and communities, election-time hostility, regionalism, social media misinformation and hate, corruption, inequality, foreign influences, materialism", "marks": 5}, {"point": "Ways (institutional): apolitical institutions (NC, monarchy above politics), ECB rules on campaigns, education on values and civic duties, strong anti-corruption, inclusive development, national service (Gyalsung, Desuung)", "marks": 6}, {"point": "Ways (personal): vote responsibly, respect differences, unity after elections, loyalty and service, avoid divisive social media, uphold Constitution and values", "marks": 6}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'tsawa-sum-and-national-unity'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 20,
