  values ('ME', 'analytical', 'Under the Tourism Levy Bill 2022, tourists visiting Bhutan will pay USD 200 per night as the Sustainable Development Fee, revised from USD 65. In addition, tourists can now choose their own operators and plan their itineraries. Discuss the implications of these changes for the tourism sector. (20 marks)', 'Changes: the Sustainable Development Fee rose from USD 65 to USD 200 per person per night, and the old minimum daily package rate (USD 200-250 that included a mandatory tour package) was removed - tourists can now choose their own hotels and operators, plan their own itineraries and pay for services directly, while hotels and guides must meet new standards.
Positive implications: (1) the policy targets high-value, low-volume tourism, protecting Bhutan''s environment, culture and sacred sites; (2) more revenue per tourist goes to the SDF, which funds conservation, health, education and carbon-offsetting; (3) flexibility attracts independent and higher-spending travellers who want customised experiences; (4) competition among operators and hotels should raise service quality; (5) hotels and service providers can sell directly to tourists.
Negative implications: (1) the higher fee made Bhutan more expensive and reduced arrivals sharply after reopening, hurting hotels, tour operators, guides, drivers, restaurants and handicraft businesses already weakened by COVID; (2) operators lose their guaranteed role, and many small agencies may close; (3) job losses pushed many tourism workers to seek work abroad; (4) Bhutan becomes less competitive against cheaper Himalayan destinations; (5) regional tourists, previously exempt, now pay a fee, reducing their numbers. The policy therefore needs monitoring and adjustment - as later shown by reducing the fee to USD 100 from 2023 - and support for affected businesses.', null, '[{"point": "Explains changes: SDF USD 65 to USD 200; end of minimum daily package rate (MDPR); tourists free to choose operators/ services and plan itineraries; certification and standards", "marks": 4}, {"point": "Positive implications: high value low volume, protects environment/ culture, more revenue per tourist, funds development, flexibility for tourists, competition raises quality, direct bookings with hotels", "marks": 8}, {"point": "Negative implications: fewer arrivals, loss of income for operators/ guides/ hotels, small operators may close, unemployment and emigration, competition from cheaper destinations, regional tourists affected, adjustment needed", "marks": 8}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'bhutan-institutions-development') and t.slug = 'tourism-policy'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-general-3'), 'standard', 20,
    2160, 'historical_official', 'awaiting_review',
    null, null,
